//@ expect: accept
//@ reason: a constructor storing its parameter into a field is at least as common
//@ reason: as a method doing so -- SmartPointerAnalyzer::promoteParamsStoredInSharedFields
//@ reason: walks constructor bodies the same way it walks method bodies, so `x`
//@ reason: stays usable after being passed into `new Chain(x)`.
//
// Chain.link is self-typed (Rule B fires, same reasoning as Node.link in the
// method-storage fixture). Single-parameter constructor (CeeLing does not support
// constructor overloading -- a separate, pre-existing limitation, not something
// this fixture exercises): the constructor's own parameter IS the field being
// linked to, so `new Chain(x)` both constructs a Chain and links it to `x` in one
// step, proving constructor bodies get the same parameter-storage promotion
// method bodies do.

class Chain {
public:
    Chain link;
    Chain(Chain l) {
        link = l;
    }
};

int main() {
    Chain x = new Chain(nullptr);
    Chain w = new Chain(x);
    Chain v = new Chain(x);   // x still usable: constructor storage doesn't consume it
    return 0;
}
